  "body": "Follow-up to #1536 and PR #1546. With #1546, a STEP whose NURBS face the tessellator cannot converge on is **refused by name in about 3 s** instead of hanging. That is safe, but the model still cannot be used.\n\n**The case:** a 30 mm cube with every edge filleted about 2-3 mm, exported from SolidCraft 0.41.0 as STEP (sha256 08c498b5…). PR #1546's repro on d569372f refuses it: \"face #836 (NURBS surface, face 9 of 62 in shell #3148) could not be tessellated ... did not converge within 25000000 evaluations ... re-export the model, or import an STL\". The faces that fail are the B-spline corner blends where three fillets meet, which is a very common shape: any part with all edges rounded has them. The same model imports as an STL from SolidCraft without trouble (51,540 triangles at about 0.003 mm chord).\n\n**Expected:** CamCraft tessellates these blends (they are well-formed in the file: closed solid, volume 19549.71 mm³ matches SolidCraft), within the facet and time budgets, so ordinary rounded parts import from STEP.\n\n**Notes:** #1546 found that truck's `sub_parameter_division` does not converge on non-continuous B-spline/NURBS faces. Options: our own adaptive or uniform parametric tessellator for B-spline faces (bounded by chord error and an evaluation cap); knot-span splitting at the discontinuities before division; or a fallback grid at the chord tolerance when division fails, judged against the face's bounds. Record the choice and its literature in a decision file.\n\n**Done means:** the filleted-cube STEP imports, and its mesh volume is within the chord tolerance of the STEP's stated volume. A pin uses a minimal STEP with one three-fillet corner blend. Faces that still cannot be meshed keep #1546's by-name refusal.\n\nFound on a user's real part (the h5/sc_logo HAT project). Related: #1536, #1545 (edge and swept divisions unmetered).\n",
